THE SHAPE (neutral reproducer, run on the tree, perl 5.40.3 vs PCL):

    use v5.36;
    sub gen ($n) {
        state $calls = 10;
        sub peek { $calls }
        $calls += $n;
        return peek();
    }
    say gen(3); say gen(4);

    perl: 13
          17
    PCL : 3
          7          (exit 0, nothing on stderr)

    Control: the same sub WITHOUT the signature (`sub gen { my $n = shift; ... }`): perl 13 17, PCL 13 17.  A `my` (not state) of a signature sub read by a nested named sub is right too (probed: `my @seq = (1, 2); sub cnt { scalar(@seq) }` = 2 in both).

WHERE (emitted Lisp, the array spelling of 0710): the signature sub is emitted by the v1 path (`;;` source comments, `(p-check-arity ...)`) and declares the state cell `(p-defcell @state__gen__seq__1 ...)`, used by gen's own statements, while the nested sub's read compiles to `(p-aref @seq ...)` -- the package global, a second name for what perl has as one pad entry.  Two resolvers for one variable (CLAUDE.md: a detector and its rewriter share ONE resolver).

CHEAP DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT (taken): the signature / no-signature pair above is the discriminator, and on the program itself: 0710 with its `<...>` import list spelled qw (#650), its `\K` table helper rewritten (#5560) AND its one signature replaced by `my $n = shift;` is byte-identical to perl; with only the first two patched it still prints a column of 1s (the nested generators read an empty package array) -- this task is that third cause.

